Consider the following testcase:

    <!DOCTYPE html>
    <script>
      function doSubmit(form) {
        form.submit();
        form.target = "";
        return true;
      }
    </script>
    <form action="http://software.hixie.ch/utilities/cgi/test-tools/echo"
          method="GET" onsubmit="return doSubmit(this)"
          target="_blank">
      <input type="submit" value="Submit me and see whether a new window/tab opens">
    </form>
  
Per spec as currently written, clicking that submit button lands in https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-control-infrastructure.html#concept-form-submit with the button as submitter.  We proceed to step 6 and fire a submit event on the form.  The submit event runs and calls the `submit()` method, which reenters the above algorithm.  This time the "submitted from submit()" flag is set, so we press on to step 17, which does the "rules for choosing a browsing context" bit.  Then it queues up the submission.  Now we unwind, see that the submit event was not canceled, press on in the original algorithm (now with `target=""` ) and queue up a new submission, removing the old one from the queue.

All good, but "rules for choosing a browsing context" has side-effects: it creates new browsing contexts.  In practice that means creating a new tab or window.  I just checked Chrome, Safari, and Firefox, and none of them do that.  I can't test Edge very easily on this, unfortunately.

